{"v":"3.0.0","project":{"accession":"PRJEB39038","title":"Microbiome signature in post-treatment Lyme disease syndrome patients","description":"Lyme disease is the most common vector-borne disease in the United States with an estimated incidence of 300,000 infections annually. Antibiotic intervention cures Lyme disease in the majority of cases; however, 10-20% of patients develop post-treatment Lyme disease syndrome (PTLDS), a debilitating condition characterized by chronic fatigue, pain, and cognitive difficulties. The underlying mechanism responsible for PTLDS symptoms, as well as a reliable diagnostic tool, has remained elusive. We reasoned that the gut microbiome may play an important role in PTLDS given that the symptoms overlap considerably with conditions in which a dysbiotic microbiome has been observed, including mood, cognition, and autoimmune disorders. Analysis of sequencing data from a rigorously curated cohort of PTLDS patients revealed a gut microbiome signature distinct from healthy control subjects, as well as from ICU patients being treated with antibiotics. Notably, microbiome sequencing data alone was indicative of PTLDS which presents a potential, novel diagnostic tool for PTLDS.","organism":null,"data_type":"Other","center_name":"University of California San Diego Microbiome Initiative","submission_date":"2020/09/08 00:00","last_update":null},"sample_cols":["accession","title","collection_date","country","region","city","lat","lon","urban_status","urban_context","eligibility_reason","isolation_source","env_broad_scale","env_local_scale","env_medium","host","location_resolution","country_iso2","place_id","location_status","location_method","location_note","geo_loc_raw","collection_year"],"samples":[["SAMEA7014624","Sample from Homo sapiens",null,null,null,null,null,null,"uncertain",null,"no environmental-sample evidence",null,null,null,null,null,"unknown",null,null,"unresolved",null,null,null,null],["SAMEA7014625","Sample from Schizosaccharomyce